1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,577
Your grandmother never threw anything away.

2
00:00:02,757 --> 00:00:05,091
You probably noticed it as a child.

3
00:00:05,271 --> 00:00:07,476
The drawer full of rubber bands.

4
00:00:07,656 --> 00:00:09,595
The coffee cans of buttons.

5
00:00:09,775 --> 00:00:12,278
The bread bags folded into neat little

6
00:00:12,278 --> 00:00:12,805
squares.

7
00:00:12,985 --> 00:00:15,017
It may have seemed odd back then.

8
00:00:15,197 --> 00:00:17,289
But every one of those habits had

9
00:00:17,289 --> 00:00:19,317
a reason, and most of them saved

10
00:00:19,317 --> 00:00:20,712
her family real money.

11
00:00:21,061 --> 00:00:23,353
The women who ran American homes in

12
00:00:23,353 --> 00:00:25,906
nineteen fifty-five had grown up in the

13
00:00:25,906 --> 00:00:29,049
Great Depression and lived through the rationing

14
00:00:29,049 --> 00:00:29,769
of the war.

15
00:00:29,949 --> 00:00:31,378
They knew what it was to have

16
00:00:31,378 --> 00:00:31,772
nothing.

17
00:00:31,952 --> 00:00:34,158
So even when times got better,

18
00:00:34,158 --> 00:00:35,702
they kept the habits.

19
00:00:35,882 --> 00:00:38,385
And they could stretch one paycheck further

20
00:00:38,385 --> 00:00:39,783
than anyone alive today.

21
00:00:40,133 --> 00:00:41,641
In nineteen fifty-five,

22
00:00:41,641 --> 00:00:44,198
the typical family earned a little over

23
00:00:44,198 --> 00:00:46,100
four thousand dollars a year.

24
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:48,305
Food took a much bigger share of

25
00:00:48,305 --> 00:00:50,204
that money than it does today.

26
00:00:50,384 --> 00:00:52,564
So every penny saved in the kitchen

27
00:00:52,564 --> 00:00:54,556
was a penny that could go toward

28
00:00:54,556 --> 00:00:56,425
the mortgage, the car payment,

29
00:00:56,425 --> 00:00:57,919
or the children's shoes.

30
00:00:58,269 --> 00:01:01,939
Tonight, let's open grandma's kitchen drawers and

31
00:01:01,939 --> 00:01:03,586
go through her tricks,

32
00:01:03,586 --> 00:01:04,410
one by one.

33
00:01:04,590 --> 00:01:08,882
Twenty-one money-saving habits from nineteen fifty-five that

34
00:01:08,882 --> 00:01:10,813
almost nobody uses anymore.

35
00:01:10,993 --> 00:01:12,444
Some of them are funny.

36
00:01:12,624 --> 00:01:14,041
Some are brilliant.

37
00:01:14,221 --> 00:01:16,167
And a few might be worth bringing

38
00:01:16,167 --> 00:01:16,462
back.

39
00:01:16,812 --> 00:01:17,671
Number one.

40
00:01:17,851 --> 00:01:19,337
The bacon grease can.

41
00:01:19,517 --> 00:01:21,422
Next to every stove sat a tin

42
00:01:21,422 --> 00:01:23,261
can or a little crock with a

43
00:01:23,261 --> 00:01:24,312
strainer on top.

44
00:01:24,492 --> 00:01:27,067
Every drop of bacon fat went into

45
00:01:27,067 --> 00:01:27,301
it.

46
00:01:27,481 --> 00:01:29,868
Grandma used it to fry eggs,

47
00:01:29,868 --> 00:01:31,743
to season green beans,

48
00:01:31,743 --> 00:01:33,277
to make cornbread,

49
00:01:33,277 --> 00:01:35,237
and to grease the pans.

50
00:01:35,417 --> 00:01:37,986
Nobody bought shortening for frying when the

51
00:01:37,986 --> 00:01:39,271
bacon grease was free.

52
00:01:39,622 --> 00:01:40,539
Number two.

53
00:01:40,719 --> 00:01:42,797
One chicken, three meals.

54
00:01:42,977 --> 00:01:45,728
On Sunday, a roast chicken for dinner.

55
00:01:45,908 --> 00:01:49,072
On Monday, the leftover meat became chicken

56
00:01:49,072 --> 00:01:51,574
salad or creamed chicken on toast.

57
00:01:51,754 --> 00:01:52,850
And on Tuesday,

58
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:55,334
the bones and the scraps went into

59
00:01:55,334 --> 00:01:57,380
a pot with an onion and some

60
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:00,740
carrots, and simmered all afternoon into soup.

61
00:02:01,091 --> 00:02:02,159
Number three.

62
00:02:02,339 --> 00:02:04,870
Stale bread was never thrown out.

63
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:07,364
It became bread pudding with raisins and

64
00:02:07,364 --> 00:02:08,405
a little cinnamon.

65
00:02:08,585 --> 00:02:11,069
Or French toast on Saturday morning.

66
00:02:11,249 --> 00:02:13,519
Or breadcrumbs for the meatloaf,

67
00:02:13,519 --> 00:02:15,788
dried in a slow oven and crushed

68
00:02:15,788 --> 00:02:17,136
with a rolling pin.

69
00:02:17,316 --> 00:02:18,728
In a careful kitchen,

70
00:02:18,728 --> 00:02:20,207
nothing went to waste.

71
00:02:20,557 --> 00:02:23,378
Leftovers had a schedule all their own.

72
00:02:23,558 --> 00:02:27,058
Sunday's roast became Monday's hash and Wednesday's

73
00:02:27,058 --> 00:02:27,401
stew.

74
00:02:27,581 --> 00:02:31,116
Leftover mashed potatoes became potato cakes,

75
00:02:31,116 --> 00:02:33,629
fried in that same bacon grease.

76
00:02:33,809 --> 00:02:36,519
A spoonful of peas went into the

77
00:02:36,519 --> 00:02:37,281
soup pot.

78
00:02:37,461 --> 00:02:40,447
The refrigerator was full of little glass

79
00:02:40,447 --> 00:02:41,685
dishes with lids,

80
00:02:41,685 --> 00:02:44,962
each holding something waiting for its second

81
00:02:44,962 --> 00:02:45,472
chance.

82
00:02:45,821 --> 00:02:46,820
Number four.

83
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,949
Meatloaf stretched with oatmeal or crackers.

84
00:02:50,129 --> 00:02:52,492
A pound of ground beef could feed

85
00:02:52,492 --> 00:02:54,497
a family of six if you mixed

86
00:02:54,497 --> 00:02:56,144
in enough bread crumbs,

87
00:02:56,144 --> 00:02:57,791
oats and a little milk.

88
00:02:57,971 --> 00:03:00,869
Grandma's meatloaf may have been more cracker

89
00:03:00,869 --> 00:03:01,513
than meat.

90
00:03:01,693 --> 00:03:04,059
But with a stripe of ketchup across

91
00:03:04,059 --> 00:03:05,884
the top, nobody complained.

92
00:03:06,234 --> 00:03:07,360
Number five.

93
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:10,593
The vegetable garden and the canning jars.

94
00:03:10,773 --> 00:03:12,104
In the summer,

95
00:03:12,104 --> 00:03:14,195
grandma grew tomatoes,

96
00:03:14,195 --> 00:03:16,857
beans, cucumbers and squash.

97
00:03:17,037 --> 00:03:19,729
In August, the kitchen turned into a

98
00:03:19,729 --> 00:03:22,048
steam bath as she put up dozens

99
00:03:22,048 --> 00:03:23,095
of Mason jars,

100
00:03:23,095 --> 00:03:26,162
lined up on basement shelves like jewels.

101
00:03:26,342 --> 00:03:29,032
Peaches, pickles, tomatoes,

102
00:03:29,032 --> 00:03:29,430
jam.

103
00:03:29,610 --> 00:03:31,654
Enough to last the whole winter.

104
00:03:32,004 --> 00:03:34,442
Canning day was often a family affair.

105
00:03:34,622 --> 00:03:37,531
The children washed the jars and snapped

106
00:03:37,531 --> 00:03:39,567
the beans on the back porch.

107
00:03:39,747 --> 00:03:42,105
Grandma worked the big pot on the

108
00:03:42,105 --> 00:03:42,534
stove.

109
00:03:42,714 --> 00:03:43,800
And all evening,

110
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,379
the kitchen was full of little pinging

111
00:03:46,379 --> 00:03:48,619
sounds, as the lids sealed one by

112
00:03:48,619 --> 00:03:48,890
one.

113
00:03:49,070 --> 00:03:51,181
Every ping meant one more jar safe

114
00:03:51,181 --> 00:03:52,112
for the winter.

115
00:03:52,463 --> 00:03:53,531
Number six.

116
00:03:53,711 --> 00:03:55,930
Powdered milk mixed in with the real

117
00:03:55,930 --> 00:03:56,300
thing.

118
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:58,844
Lots of families quietly mixed a pitcher

119
00:03:58,844 --> 00:04:00,973
of powdered milk into the fresh milk

120
00:04:00,973 --> 00:04:02,273
to make it go further.

121
00:04:02,453 --> 00:04:04,113
The children noticed the taste.

122
00:04:04,293 --> 00:04:06,268
The mothers pretended they didn't know what

123
00:04:06,268 --> 00:04:07,370
they were talking about.

124
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:08,799
Number seven.

125
00:04:08,979 --> 00:04:10,442
Nothing was disposable.

126
00:04:10,622 --> 00:04:12,901
Aluminum foil was washed,

127
00:04:12,901 --> 00:04:15,545
smoothed flat and used again.

128
00:04:15,725 --> 00:04:19,435
Glass jars became drinking glasses and storage

129
00:04:19,435 --> 00:04:20,322
containers.

130
00:04:20,502 --> 00:04:22,813
Bread bags held sandwiches.

131
00:04:22,993 --> 00:04:26,524
Paper grocery sacks became book covers for

132
00:04:26,524 --> 00:04:28,121
school, trash bags,

133
00:04:28,121 --> 00:04:30,307
and wrapping for packages.

134
00:04:30,487 --> 00:04:33,035
Even string was wound into a ball

135
00:04:33,035 --> 00:04:33,807
and saved.

136
00:04:34,158 --> 00:04:34,993
Number eight.

137
00:04:35,173 --> 00:04:36,404
The button jar.

138
00:04:36,584 --> 00:04:39,134
Before an old shirt became a rag,

139
00:04:39,134 --> 00:04:42,225
grandma cut off every button and dropped

140
00:04:42,225 --> 00:04:43,306
it into a jar.

141
00:04:43,486 --> 00:04:45,500
When a button popped off a coat,

142
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:47,074
she didn't buy a new one.

143
00:04:47,254 --> 00:04:49,417
She poured the jar onto the table

144
00:04:49,417 --> 00:04:50,597
and found a match.

145
00:04:50,777 --> 00:04:53,579
Children loved sorting through that jar on

146
00:04:53,579 --> 00:04:54,713
rainy afternoons.

147
00:04:55,064 --> 00:04:56,027
Number nine.

148
00:04:56,207 --> 00:04:57,438
The rag bag.

149
00:04:57,618 --> 00:05:00,253
Worn-out clothes were never thrown away.

150
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:02,534
They went into a cloth bag hanging

151
00:05:02,534 --> 00:05:03,893
on the back of a door.

152
00:05:04,073 --> 00:05:06,650
Old towels became dust cloths.

153
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:10,093
Old sheets became bandages and dish towels.

154
00:05:10,273 --> 00:05:12,595
Old wool became braided rugs.

155
00:05:12,775 --> 00:05:14,612
Old dresses became quilts,

156
00:05:14,612 --> 00:05:17,156
pieced together by hand over a whole

157
00:05:17,156 --> 00:05:17,651
winter.

158
00:05:18,001 --> 00:05:20,822
Quilting was also how women got together.

159
00:05:21,002 --> 00:05:23,871
A quilting bee in the church basement

160
00:05:23,871 --> 00:05:25,887
or somebody's living room,

161
00:05:25,887 --> 00:05:28,679
a dozen women around a wooden frame,

162
00:05:28,679 --> 00:05:31,160
stitching and talking for hours.

163
00:05:31,340 --> 00:05:33,439
They finished a quilt in a day,

164
00:05:33,439 --> 00:05:35,538
and the best gossip in town was

165
00:05:35,538 --> 00:05:37,366
shared over those stitches.

166
00:05:37,716 --> 00:05:38,656
Number ten.

167
00:05:38,836 --> 00:05:41,529
Mending, darning and patching.

168
00:05:41,709 --> 00:05:44,209
Grandma kept a wooden darning egg in

169
00:05:44,209 --> 00:05:45,459
her sewing basket.

170
00:05:45,639 --> 00:05:47,870
When a sock wore through at the

171
00:05:47,870 --> 00:05:50,245
heel, she slipped it over the egg

172
00:05:50,245 --> 00:05:52,476
and wove a new heel with needle

173
00:05:52,476 --> 00:05:53,267
and thread.

174
00:05:53,447 --> 00:05:55,177
Elbows got patches.

175
00:05:55,357 --> 00:05:57,422
Collars were turned inside out to hide

176
00:05:57,422 --> 00:05:57,911
the wear.

177
00:05:58,091 --> 00:06:00,088
Clothes lasted for years.

178
00:06:00,439 --> 00:06:01,576
Number eleven.

179
00:06:01,756 --> 00:06:04,160
Sewing the family's clothes at home.

180
00:06:04,340 --> 00:06:06,657
Many women sewed their own dresses and

181
00:06:06,657 --> 00:06:09,096
their children's clothes on a treadle or

182
00:06:09,096 --> 00:06:10,133
electric machine.

183
00:06:10,313 --> 00:06:13,087
They bought patterns at the five-and-dime for

184
00:06:13,087 --> 00:06:13,703
a quarter.

185
00:06:13,883 --> 00:06:16,417
And some flour and feed sacks came

186
00:06:16,417 --> 00:06:18,803
printed with flowers and checks,

187
00:06:18,803 --> 00:06:21,337
so farm women could turn them into

188
00:06:21,337 --> 00:06:22,753
dresses and aprons.

189
00:06:23,103 --> 00:06:24,159
Number twelve.

190
00:06:24,339 --> 00:06:27,044
Hand-me-downs, all the way down.

191
00:06:27,224 --> 00:06:29,616
The oldest child got the new coat.

192
00:06:29,796 --> 00:06:30,778
Two years later,

193
00:06:30,778 --> 00:06:32,373
it went to the next child.

194
00:06:32,553 --> 00:06:33,412
Then the next.

195
00:06:33,592 --> 00:06:36,058
By the time it reached the youngest,

196
00:06:36,058 --> 00:06:37,428
it had been let out,

197
00:06:37,428 --> 00:06:40,442
taken in, patched and re-hemmed three times.

198
00:06:40,622 --> 00:06:43,664
And cousins traded boxes of outgrown clothes

199
00:06:43,664 --> 00:06:45,185
at every family visit.

200
00:06:45,535 --> 00:06:47,217
Shoes were resoled,

201
00:06:47,217 --> 00:06:48,367
not replaced.

202
00:06:48,547 --> 00:06:51,366
The cobbler downtown put new heels and

203
00:06:51,366 --> 00:06:53,665
soles on the same pair of shoes

204
00:06:53,665 --> 00:06:54,852
again and again.

205
00:06:55,032 --> 00:06:57,713
Children's shoes were bought a size too

206
00:06:57,713 --> 00:07:00,188
big, stuffed with tissue in the toe,

207
00:07:00,188 --> 00:07:01,220
and grown into.

208
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:03,111
And every Saturday night,

209
00:07:03,111 --> 00:07:06,191
fathers polished the whole family's shoes for

210
00:07:06,191 --> 00:07:06,671
church.

211
00:07:07,021 --> 00:07:08,379
Number thirteen.

212
00:07:08,559 --> 00:07:10,103
Turning off the lights.

213
00:07:10,283 --> 00:07:11,755
If you left a room with the

214
00:07:11,755 --> 00:07:13,336
light on, you heard about it.

215
00:07:13,516 --> 00:07:16,764
Grandpa walked through the house every evening,

216
00:07:16,764 --> 00:07:19,252
switching off lamps behind everyone.

217
00:07:19,432 --> 00:07:21,392
Do you think we own the electric

218
00:07:21,392 --> 00:07:21,881
company?

219
00:07:22,061 --> 00:07:25,083
Every child in America heard that sentence

220
00:07:25,083 --> 00:07:26,090
at least once.

221
00:07:26,441 --> 00:07:27,706
Number fourteen.

222
00:07:27,886 --> 00:07:29,987
Hanging the laundry outside.

223
00:07:30,167 --> 00:07:32,737
Even families with a new electric dryer

224
00:07:32,737 --> 00:07:34,912
often hung their wash on the line

225
00:07:34,912 --> 00:07:35,966
in good weather,

226
00:07:35,966 --> 00:07:37,679
because sunshine was free.

227
00:07:37,859 --> 00:07:40,578
Sheets dried stiff and smelled like fresh

228
00:07:40,578 --> 00:07:40,843
air.

229
00:07:41,023 --> 00:07:44,129
And wooden clothespins lasted for decades,

230
00:07:44,129 --> 00:07:46,347
kept in a cloth bag hung right

231
00:07:46,347 --> 00:07:47,234
on the line.

232
00:07:47,585 --> 00:07:48,838
Number fifteen.

233
00:07:49,018 --> 00:07:51,201
Homemade cleaning supplies.

234
00:07:51,381 --> 00:07:53,584
Vinegar and water for the windows,

235
00:07:53,584 --> 00:07:56,369
wiped with crumpled newspaper so there were

236
00:07:56,369 --> 00:07:57,082
no streaks.

237
00:07:57,262 --> 00:07:59,260
Baking soda for the sink and the

238
00:07:59,260 --> 00:07:59,572
oven.

239
00:07:59,752 --> 00:08:01,656
Lemon juice for stains.

240
00:08:01,836 --> 00:08:03,933
A bar of plain soap for nearly

241
00:08:03,933 --> 00:08:05,052
everything else.

242
00:08:05,232 --> 00:08:07,890
One box of baking soda could clean

243
00:08:07,890 --> 00:08:09,063
half the house.

244
00:08:09,414 --> 00:08:11,306
Haircuts happened at home too.

245
00:08:11,486 --> 00:08:13,974
Many mothers cut the children's hair at

246
00:08:13,974 --> 00:08:15,123
the kitchen table,

247
00:08:15,123 --> 00:08:17,164
with a towel around the neck and

248
00:08:17,164 --> 00:08:18,313
a bowl as a guide.

249
00:08:18,493 --> 00:08:20,501
The results were not always beautiful.

250
00:08:20,681 --> 00:08:22,157
But a barber cost money,

251
00:08:22,157 --> 00:08:23,386
and a bowl was free.

252
00:08:23,566 --> 00:08:26,011
Every family album has at least one

253
00:08:26,011 --> 00:08:27,618
photograph to prove it.

254
00:08:27,969 --> 00:08:29,257
Number sixteen.

255
00:08:29,437 --> 00:08:31,330
Making do with one of everything.

256
00:08:31,510 --> 00:08:32,636
One bathroom.

257
00:08:32,816 --> 00:08:33,965
One telephone.

258
00:08:34,145 --> 00:08:37,419
One radio, and later one television.

259
00:08:37,599 --> 00:08:38,574
One car.

260
00:08:38,754 --> 00:08:41,135
Most families didn't feel deprived.

261
00:08:41,315 --> 00:08:43,516
It was simply how everybody lived,

262
00:08:43,516 --> 00:08:45,718
and the money that wasn't spent on

263
00:08:45,718 --> 00:08:48,243
extras went into the savings account at

264
00:08:48,243 --> 00:08:48,826
the bank.

265
00:08:49,177 --> 00:08:50,407
Number seventeen.

266
00:08:50,587 --> 00:08:53,025
The coin purse and cash only.

267
00:08:53,205 --> 00:08:56,653
Grandma paid for almost everything in cash.

268
00:08:56,833 --> 00:08:58,610
If she didn't have the money,

269
00:08:58,610 --> 00:09:00,142
she didn't buy the thing.

270
00:09:00,322 --> 00:09:04,061
Credit cards hardly existed in nineteen fifty-five.

271
00:09:04,241 --> 00:09:06,275
When people did buy on credit,

272
00:09:06,275 --> 00:09:08,309
it was usually a big item from

273
00:09:08,309 --> 00:09:09,868
a store that knew them,

274
00:09:09,868 --> 00:09:11,767
and they paid it off as fast

275
00:09:11,767 --> 00:09:12,716
as they could.

276
00:09:13,065 --> 00:09:14,343
Number eighteen.

277
00:09:14,523 --> 00:09:15,660
The layaway plan.

278
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:17,724
If you wanted a winter coat or

279
00:09:17,724 --> 00:09:19,231
a bicycle for Christmas,

280
00:09:19,231 --> 00:09:21,114
the store put it aside for you

281
00:09:21,114 --> 00:09:22,307
behind the counter.

282
00:09:22,487 --> 00:09:24,802
You paid a little every week until

283
00:09:24,802 --> 00:09:26,163
it was paid in full,

284
00:09:26,163 --> 00:09:28,137
and only then did you take it

285
00:09:28,137 --> 00:09:28,478
home.

286
00:09:28,658 --> 00:09:29,761
No interest.

287
00:09:29,941 --> 00:09:30,603
No debt.

288
00:09:30,783 --> 00:09:31,955
Just patience.

289
00:09:32,305 --> 00:09:34,697
The Christmas club worked the same way.

290
00:09:34,877 --> 00:09:37,836
In January, you opened a special account

291
00:09:37,836 --> 00:09:39,907
at the bank and put in fifty

292
00:09:39,907 --> 00:09:42,052
cents or a dollar every week.

293
00:09:42,232 --> 00:09:43,292
In late November,

294
00:09:43,292 --> 00:09:45,224
the bank mailed you a check for

295
00:09:45,224 --> 00:09:46,284
the whole amount.

296
00:09:46,464 --> 00:09:48,549
That check paid for Christmas,

297
00:09:48,549 --> 00:09:50,911
and nobody started the new year in

298
00:09:50,911 --> 00:09:51,259
debt.

299
00:09:51,608 --> 00:09:52,816
Number nineteen.

300
00:09:52,996 --> 00:09:54,122
Trading stamps.

301
00:09:54,302 --> 00:09:57,040
Every purchase at the supermarket or the

302
00:09:57,040 --> 00:09:59,503
gas station came with little stamps.

303
00:09:59,683 --> 00:10:01,967
Green ones, or gold ones,

304
00:10:01,967 --> 00:10:03,155
or blue ones.

305
00:10:03,335 --> 00:10:05,533
Grandma saved them in a drawer,

306
00:10:05,533 --> 00:10:08,158
and on a rainy afternoon the children

307
00:10:08,158 --> 00:10:11,137
licked them and pasted them into booklets.

308
00:10:11,317 --> 00:10:13,556
A few dozen books might buy a

309
00:10:13,556 --> 00:10:15,641
toaster, a lamp or a set of

310
00:10:15,641 --> 00:10:16,181
dishes.

311
00:10:16,531 --> 00:10:17,553
Number twenty.

312
00:10:17,733 --> 00:10:20,218
Coupons and the Wednesday newspaper.

313
00:10:20,398 --> 00:10:24,009
Grocery stores published their weekly specials in

314
00:10:24,009 --> 00:10:27,399
the newspaper, often on Wednesday or Thursday.

315
00:10:27,579 --> 00:10:29,973
Grandma read the ads with a pencil,

316
00:10:29,973 --> 00:10:32,710
planned the week's meals around what was

317
00:10:32,710 --> 00:10:35,037
on sale, and sometimes went to two

318
00:10:35,037 --> 00:10:37,500
or three different stores to get the

319
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:39,142
best price on each item.

320
00:10:39,491 --> 00:10:41,616
And grandma knew her grocer.

321
00:10:41,796 --> 00:10:44,195
If she bought a little extra flour

322
00:10:44,195 --> 00:10:45,465
when it was cheap,

323
00:10:45,465 --> 00:10:48,076
he might set aside the bruised apples

324
00:10:48,076 --> 00:10:49,981
for her pies at half price.

325
00:10:50,161 --> 00:10:52,139
If the family hit a hard month,

326
00:10:52,139 --> 00:10:54,054
many corner grocers ran a tab,

327
00:10:54,054 --> 00:10:56,543
written in a little notebook behind the

328
00:10:56,543 --> 00:10:58,713
counter, and trusted her to pay it

329
00:10:58,713 --> 00:10:59,925
off when she could.

330
00:11:00,275 --> 00:11:01,599
And number twenty-one.

331
00:11:01,779 --> 00:11:04,403
The cookie tin that never held cookies.

332
00:11:04,583 --> 00:11:06,278
Every grandmother had one.

333
00:11:06,458 --> 00:11:08,836
A round blue tin that looked like

334
00:11:08,836 --> 00:11:10,997
it was full of butter cookies.

335
00:11:11,177 --> 00:11:13,450
Inside, it held thread,

336
00:11:13,450 --> 00:11:16,019
needles, buttons and pins.

337
00:11:16,199 --> 00:11:19,404
Or sometimes, tucked at the very bottom,

338
00:11:19,404 --> 00:11:21,888
a few folded bills saved for an

339
00:11:21,888 --> 00:11:22,689
emergency.

340
00:11:22,869 --> 00:11:25,028
You never knew until you opened it.

341
00:11:25,378 --> 00:11:27,413
It's easy to laugh at some of

342
00:11:27,413 --> 00:11:28,606
these habits now.

343
00:11:28,786 --> 00:11:30,546
But think about what they added up

344
00:11:30,546 --> 00:11:30,702
to.

345
00:11:30,882 --> 00:11:31,967
A family of five,

346
00:11:31,967 --> 00:11:33,883
living on one modest paycheck,

347
00:11:33,883 --> 00:11:35,032
owning their home,

348
00:11:35,032 --> 00:11:37,012
with money in the bank and food

349
00:11:37,012 --> 00:11:38,927
on the shelves for the winter.

350
00:11:39,107 --> 00:11:40,942
That didn't happen by accident.

351
00:11:41,122 --> 00:11:43,861
It happened because somebody in that house

352
00:11:43,861 --> 00:11:45,557
was very, very good at it.

353
00:11:45,906 --> 00:11:48,960
Those women never called it budgeting,

354
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:50,648
or saving the planet,

355
00:11:50,648 --> 00:11:52,095
or reducing waste.

356
00:11:52,275 --> 00:11:54,109
They called it common sense.

357
00:11:54,289 --> 00:11:55,218
Use it up,

358
00:11:55,218 --> 00:11:56,332
wear it out,

359
00:11:56,332 --> 00:11:57,354
make it do,

360
00:11:57,354 --> 00:11:58,654
or do without.

361
00:11:58,834 --> 00:12:00,807
That old saying hung in a lot

362
00:12:00,807 --> 00:12:02,236
of American kitchens,

363
00:12:02,236 --> 00:12:04,822
sometimes stitched on a sampler on the

364
00:12:04,822 --> 00:12:05,162
wall.

365
00:12:05,511 --> 00:12:07,241
So tell us in the comments.

366
00:12:07,421 --> 00:12:09,297
Which of these did your mother or

367
00:12:09,297 --> 00:12:10,150
grandmother do?

368
00:12:10,330 --> 00:12:11,340
And did we miss one?

369
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:12,883
We read every comment,

370
00:12:12,883 --> 00:12:14,804
and we'd love to hear the trick

371
00:12:14,804 --> 00:12:16,106
your family swore by.

372
00:12:16,286 --> 00:12:17,455
And stay with us,

373
00:12:17,455 --> 00:12:20,068
because next we're going to a nineteen

374
00:12:20,068 --> 00:12:21,719
fifty-five factory town.

375
00:12:21,899 --> 00:12:23,725
Thank you for spending this time with

376
00:12:23,725 --> 00:12:23,873
me.
